Description: Fix CVE-2026-73231 — block eval injection in helpers.fake()
 The fake()/fakeEval() expression resolver walked attacker-controlled dotted
 paths by dereferencing each part directly on the current entrypoint. Two
 gaps let a template escape the intended faker/definitions object graph and
 reach JavaScript intrinsics (Eval Injection, CWE-94):
 .
 1. resolveProperty() invoked a function entrypoint and then indexed the
    result with a single `entrypoint?.[key]`, which also succeeds on
    primitives. `{{helpers.slugify.constructor}}` therefore resolved
    String, and `{{location.city.constructor("x")()}}` invoked it.
 2. The dangerous prototype-chain keys were never rejected, so
    `{{location.__proto__.constructor.constructor(...)}}` walked off the
    module graph onto Object/Function.
 .
 This backports the upstream resolveProperty() recursion (faker-js/faker
 v10.5.0) so the type switch is re-evaluated after invoking a function —
 primitives now fall through to `undefined` instead of being indexed — and
 additionally refuses to traverse the `constructor`, `__proto__` and
 `prototype` keys, throwing the existing FakerError. Legitimate lookups
 (person.firstName, airline.airline().name, airline.airline.name,
 string.numeric(4), phone.number({"style":"national"}), multi-expression
 templates) are unaffected.
 .
 Retargeted at the compiled bundles shipped in the npm registry tarball
 (the ESM chunk dist/chunk-YQYVFZYE.js and the CJS chunk
 dist/chunk-6BEEDJJT.cjs, the only two chunks carrying the eval core),
 since the @faker-js/faker 9.9.0 npm package ships only compiled dist/
 output and contains no src/*.ts or test/*.spec.ts. The upstream fix
 targets src/modules/helpers/eval.ts, which does not exist in this version.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/faker-js/faker/blob/v10.5.0/src/modules/helpers/eval.ts
Bug: CVE-2026-73231
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-09-16

